## Recommendation: Use `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+)

.NET 8 ships `System.TimeProvider` — an abstract class designed exactly for this:

```csharp
public class OrderProcessor
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public OrderProcessor(TimeProvider timeProvider)
    {
        _time = timeProvider;
    }

    public Order Create() => new()
    {
        CreatedAt = _time.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime,
        ExpiresAt = _time.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime.AddDays(30)
    };
}
```

**In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var processor = new OrderProcessor(fake);

// Advance time deterministically
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(31));
Assert.True(processor.IsExpired(order));
```

**In production DI:**
```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

If you're on an older .NET, create a simple `IClock` interface with a `UtcNow` property and inject it the same way. Want me to refactor the code to apply this pattern?
